<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>

    <style name="Theme.SupportChat" parent="android:style/Theme.Material.Light.NoActionBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@color/window_background</item>
        <item name="android:statusBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightStatusBar">true</item>
    </style>

    <!--
        The launch theme. Owns the window from the launcher tap until the first composed frame,
        then hands the activity over to Theme.SupportChat through postSplashScreenTheme, which
        installSplashScreen() applies in onCreate. Nothing after that point can tell the app
        started on a different theme.

        There is deliberately no values-night copy of this style. Both values it references are
        already night-aware: @color/window_background is overridden in values-night/colors.xml,
        and @style/Theme.SupportChat is overridden in values-night/styles.xml. Duplicating the
        style here would create a second place to forget to update.

        windowSplashScreenBackground is window_background rather than a brand colour, so the
        handover to StartupScreen, which paints colorScheme.background, is invisible. Any other
        value would read as a one-frame flash of the wrong colour.

        (No double hyphens anywhere in this comment: XML forbids them inside comments, and an
        editor that let one through would break resource parsing for the whole file.)
    -->
    <style name="Theme.SupportChat.Starting" parent="Theme.SplashScreen">
        <item name="windowSplashScreenBackground">@color/window_background</item>
        <item name="windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon">@drawable/ic_splash_logo</item>
        <item name="postSplashScreenTheme">@style/Theme.SupportChat</item>
    </style>

</resources>
